---
import "../../styles/style.css";
const products = [
{
    name: "A Quiet Reverie",
    images: ["/images/student_french.jpeg"],
    description: `
    French School of the 19th Century, A Quiet Reverie, Pastel on paper (oval), 60.5 x 49 cm, Framed under glass.

    In the manner of François Boucher, this delicate pastel portrait revisits the codes of a grace inherited from the 18th century.

    The young girl, caught in a gesture of withdrawal, her gaze lowered and her hand restrained, seems absorbed in an inner gentleness.

    The vaporous modelling of the pastel, the diffused light, and the palette of tender tones lend the whole a silent, almost suspended presence.

    Far from any ostentation, the work favours intimacy: a bare shoulder, a handkerchief held without emphasis, a few folds of fabric are enough
    to suggest a world of sensation rather than a narrative.

    The frame, in gilded wood, with its supple lines and rocaille-inspired ornaments,
    extends this atmosphere by placing the image within a refined decorative tradition, in keeping with the spirit of the composition.

    2500 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Portrait of a Young Italian Woman, 1880",
    images: ["/images/student_native.jpeg"],
    description: `
    Passini, Ludwig Johann (1832 Vienna – 1903 Venice), Portrait of a Young Italian Woman, 1880, Watercolour on paper, Signed and dated upper right “Ludwig Passini 1880”, 16 x 21 cm.

    Ludwig Johann Passini (1832–1903) was an Austrian painter and draughtsman renowned for the delicacy of his portraits and the precision of his observation. 
    
    Trained in Vienna and later active in Venice, he developed a body of work attentive to faces and attitudes, favouring figures captured in a form of inner silence.
    
    In this portrait, characteristic of his approach, Passini sets aside all spectacular effects to focus on the presence of the sitter: the gaze slightly averted, 
    the softness of the light, the texture of the fabrics. His work, often executed in watercolour and drawing, is distinguished by a subtle sensitivity and a rare 
    psychological accuracy.

    Appreciated during his lifetime, he embodies an intimate realism, where the image becomes above all a place of encounter with an individual presence.

    1700 €
    `
    },
  {
    name: "Buffalo herd",
    images: ["/images/buffalo.jpeg"],
    description: `
    Jung-Ilsenheim, Franz Xaver (1883 - 1963), Buffalo herd, Gouache on paper, Signed lower left “F. Jung-Ilsenheim”, 27 x 39 cm, Framed under glass.

    Franz Xaver Jung-Ilsenheim (1883–1963) was a German painter of the 20th century, specialising in animal scenes and rural landscapes. 
    His work is distinguished by careful observation of the natural world, particularly of herds, which he depicted with a calm presence and great fidelity of character.

    In this composition, he captures a group of bison in clear light, where the solidity of the animal masses enters into dialogue with the openness of the landscape. 
    The direct, almost spontaneous treatment highlights the contrasts of light and reflections in the water, while preserving an impression of simplicity and balance.

    Through these scenes, Jung-Ilsenheim develops a painting rooted in reality, where nature is neither dramatised nor idealised, but shown in its quiet strength.

    800 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Orientalist Assault Scene",
    images: ["/images/assault_orient.jpeg"],
    description: `
    French School of the 19th Century, Orientalist Assault Scene, Pen and ink wash with watercolour over black chalk on laid paper, 24,5 x 35 cm, Framed under glass.

    From the French School of the 19th century, this orientalist assault scene belongs to the taste of an era fascinated by tales of distant lands and the dramatic tensions 
    they evoke. Executed in pen, heightened with wash and watercolour over a black chalk outline, the drawing combines precision of line with fluidity of shadow to construct 
    a lively yet legible composition.

    The artist here favours movement and narrative: the figures stand out through successive contrasts, in a rapid hand that captures the energy of the confrontation 
    without dwelling on anecdotal detail. This economy of means lends the whole a particular intensity, where gesture takes precedence over effect.

    Typical of workshop sheets or finished studies intended for preservation, the work bears witness to a gaze that is both documentary and imaginary, where the Orient 
    is less a precise location than a space for visual and narrative projection.

    350 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Sultan Ahmet Mosque (Blue Mosque), Istanbul, 1930",
    images: ["/images/mosque.jpeg"],
    description: `
    Pawlikiewicz, Jozef (1894-1930), Sultan Ahmet Mosque (Blue Mosque), Istanbul, 1930, Watercolour on paper, Signed, located and dated lower left, 43 x 28,5 cm, Framed under glass.
    
    Jozef Pawlikiewicz (1894–1930) was a Polish painter active in the early 20th century, belonging to the tradition of travelling artists. 
    Trained in Central Europe, he specialised in urban and architectural views executed in watercolour.

    In this depiction of the Sultan Ahmet Mosque in Istanbul, he renders with precision the lines of the monument and the rhythm of its minarets, while giving significant 
    place to the animated foreground — boats, figures, shoreline — that roots the scene in daily life. The watercolour, supple and swift, allows him to capture the variations 
    of light and reflections on the water, while maintaining a clear reading of the architecture.
    His work, produced largely during travels, is characterised by a direct approach to the motif, attentive both to the structure of places and to their atmosphere.
    
    500 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Entwined Couple",
    images: ["/images/entwined_couple.png"],
    description: `
    Ivanoff, Serge (1893-1983), Entwined Couple, Brown chalk on paper, Signed lower right, 24,5 x 36,5 cm, Framed under glass.

    Serge Ivanoff (1893–1983) was a Russian-born artist active in Europe during the 20th century, known for his portraits, genre scenes, and figurative compositions. 
    Trained in the academic tradition, he developed a solid draughtsmanship, giving essential place to the structure of the body and the expressiveness of line.

    In this chalk drawing depicting an entwined couple, Ivanoff favours a direct and synthetic approach: the volumes are built with a few energetic lines and dark accents, 
    without seeking superfluous detail. The medium, free and swift, captures the intensity of the gesture and the tension of the bodies within a tightly composed image.

    This work illustrates his commitment to drawing as an autonomous means of expression, where the human figure is treated with economy and precision.

    500 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Birch Trees",
    images: ["/images/birch_trees.png"],
    description: `
    Lindberg, Otto (1880 – 1955), Birch Trees, 1930, Oil on canvas, Signed and dated lower right, 55,5 x 40 cm, Framed, Frame dimensions 70 x 55 cm.

    Otto Lindberg was a painter active in the early 20th century, whose work belongs to the tradition of Nordic landscapes. He developed a painting attentive to variations 
    of light and seasonal atmospheres, with a predilection for woodland interiors and forest edges.

    In this view of birch trees dated 1930, he constructs a sober composition in which the vertical trunks punctuate the space and guide the eye into depth. The restrained, 
    lightly veiled palette conveys a calm, almost silent atmosphere, characteristic of Northern landscapes.

    His painting favours a direct approach to the motif, centred on the balance of masses and the perception of nature, without any marked narrative effect.

    400 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Chair VIII, 2025",
    images: ["/images/chair_VIII_2025.png"],
    description: `
    Hafner, Gabriel (1995 - ), Chair VIII, 2025, Wood, leather and steel, 67 x 49 x 88 cm

    Furniture and objects developed for Galerie Ida’s Room in Paris. The chairs follow a design approach inspired by adhocism, as described by Charles Jencks. 
    The project works from existing materials — discarded objects, industrial residues and standardised elements found in the urban environment. The forms emerge through 
    improvisation and iterative assembly, giving rise to functional prototypes that challenge established design conventions in a post-industrial context.

    Gabriel Hafner (born 1995) is a Swiss, Franco-German designer based in Paris. Trained in business administration at the University of St. Gallen (HSG) and then in industrial 
    design at ECAL in Lausanne, he founded his studio upon completing his studies.

    His practice sits at the intersection of use and symbol. He designs furniture, everyday objects and installations from found objects, raw materials and processes developed 
    in the workshop. His work puts the hand, the gesture and industrial logic in tension. For him, making is a way of thinking out loud. Each piece engages a function but also 
    a reading. Chair, architectural artefact or rug become forms that orient behaviours and reveal systems of value.

    His work has been exhibited in Europe, Asia and the United States. He has shown at the Vitra Design Museum in Germany, the Villa Noailles and the Musée des Arts Décoratifs 
    et du Design de Bordeaux in France, the Seoul Living Design Fair, Milan Design Week and Collectible, the fair held in New York. His pieces have entered the collection of 
    the Centre National des Arts Plastiques. In 2024, he received the Prix Tectona at the Design Parade Hyères.

    His practice is grounded in making, in the encounter with materials and in an attention to contemporary symbols.

    1200 €
    `
  },
  {
    name: "Chair XV",
    images: ["/images/chair_XV.png"],
    description: `
    Hafner, Gabriel (1995 - ), Chair XV, Reclaimed metal and chair back, 40 x 65 x 85 cm

